\description{
\strong{Difference of Averages (DA)} synthesizes each subgroup series separately
and takes the difference of those pooled treatment effects. Study
inverse-variance weights generally differ across the two subgroup
analyses, so DA need not equal \code{\link[=ad]{ad()}}. Panaro, Röver and Friede (2026)
treat DA and AD as \strong{different constructions}.
}
\details{
For binary subgroups with reference \eqn{A} and non-reference \eqn{B},
\eqn{\mu_A} and \eqn{\mu_B} are independent random-effects meta-analyses
of the subgroup series, and
\eqn{\gamma_{\mathrm{DA}} = \mu_B - \mu_A}.

DA is an \strong{analysis construction}, not an inference framework. It may be
fitted with frequentist inference (\code{engine = "metafor"}, REML/ML) or
Bayesian inference (\code{engine = "bayesmeta"}). Bayesian DA fits two independent
univariate \code{\link[bayesmeta:bayesmeta]{bayesmeta::bayesmeta()}} models and forms \eqn{\mu_B - \mu_A} by
Cartesian-product Gaussian-mixture (and optional convolution). It does
\strong{not} use quantile matching, and it does \strong{not} acquire CAMS
information-fraction functionality merely because a posterior exists.

Fisher et al. (2017) discussed a related across-trial contrast of
subgroup averages as the “deluded” approach. That paper does not define
the DA/AD pair used here. DA is \strong{not} the joint bivariate mixed model
of van Houwelingen, Arends and Stijnen (2002). That joint model remains a
related reference for modelling two subgroup series together with a
heterogeneity matrix \eqn{\Sigma}; \code{\link[=da]{da()}} uses separate univariate
pooling instead.

\code{\link[=ad]{ad()}} meta-analyses the within-trial differences. \code{\link[=swada]{swada()}} imposes the
same study weights on both constructions. \code{\link[=cam]{cam()}} is a different object:
the CAMS statistical model, which is the analysis family that uses the
information-fraction construction.
}
